    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The text traces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a>, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> until 1974, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a> through their son Menelik I.</li><li>Modern scholarship considers its stories largely created to legitimise the dynasty's rise to power in the 13th century, while many Ethiopian Christians continue to regard it as historically reliable.</li><li>Its colophon gives a chain of translation from Coptic to Arabic to Geʽez, but many scholars doubt that a Coptic version ever existed.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the story, Menelik asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> for only a single tassel from the cloth covering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant">Ark of the Covenant</a>, and Solomon gave him the entire thing; the young men travelling with him then smuggled the Ark out of the Temple.</p></aside>
    </section>

